CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ non-const member template selected: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
For a call on a non-const object, a non-const member function template must be
preferred over a const one (the implicit object parameter's cv-qualification,
[over.match.funcs]/5).  CBMC ranks this correctly for non-template members but
selects the const overload for member function templates, so a modifying use
(s.acc<int>() = ...) fails as "not an lvalue".  This underlies libstdc++
std::function's _Any_data::_M_access and _Base_manager::_M_create.
